// =====================================================================
// RULE:      Anomalous Azure OpenAI request volume
// SEVERITY:  Medium
// ATT&CK:    Impact — T1496 (Resource Hijacking)
// OWASP:     LLM10 Unbounded Consumption
// CONTROLS:  NIST 800-53 SI-4, SC-6 · NIST AI RMF MANAGE 4.1 (post-deployment
//            monitoring) · NERC CIP-007-6 R4
// SOURCE:    AzureDiagnostics  (requires diagnostic settings on the account)
// SCHEDULE:  run every 1h, look back 24h
// ENTITIES:  AzureResource = Resource · IP = CallerIPAddress (where present)
//
// PREREQUISITE — AND ITS COST
// This rule needs diagnostic settings enabled on the Cognitive Services account
// and pointed at the workspace. That adds ingestion, which is the one thing
// Sentinel bills for. For a lab: enable it, capture the evidence, disable it.
//
//   az monitor diagnostic-settings create \
//     --name seclab-oai-diag \
//     --resource "/subscriptions/<sub>/resourceGroups/rg-seclab-ai/providers/Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts/oai-seclab-lsr01" \
//     --workspace "/subscriptions/<sub>/resourceGroups/rg-seclab-siem/providers/Microsoft.OperationalInsights/workspaces/law-seclab" \
//     --logs '[{"category":"RequestResponse","enabled":true}]'
//
// WHY THIS RULE EXISTS
// Lab G rates T8 Unbounded Consumption as low residual risk precisely BECAUSE
// cost controls and quotas are effective. This rule is the detective half of
// that claim. The threat is denial of wallet: the endpoint is a public URL
// authenticated by a bearer key, so a leaked key becomes someone else's free
// inference capacity, and the first symptom is a bill.
//
// Note the relationship to detect-cognitive-key-listed.kql: key listed, then
// volume spike, is a two-event story that is far stronger than either alone.
// =====================================================================

let lookback   = 24h;
let bin_size   = 1h;
let sensitivity = 1.5;   // lower = more sensitive
AzureDiagnostics
| where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
| where ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.COGNITIVESERVICES"
| summarize Requests = count() by bin(TimeGenerated, bin_size), Resource
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| summarize
    Timestamps = make_list(TimeGenerated),
    Counts     = make_list(Requests)
    by Resource
| extend (Anomalies, Score, Baseline) =
    series_decompose_anomalies(Counts, sensitivity, -1, 'linefit')
| mv-expand Timestamps to typeof(datetime),
            Counts     to typeof(long),
            Anomalies  to typeof(int),
            Score      to typeof(double),
            Baseline   to typeof(long)
| where Anomalies > 0
| project
    TimeGenerated = Timestamps,
    Resource,
    Requests      = Counts,
    Expected      = Baseline,
    AnomalyScore  = round(Score, 2)
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// A HONEST CAVEAT ABOUT ANOMALY DETECTION ON A LAB
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// series_decompose_anomalies needs a baseline, and a lab that sees traffic
// twice a week has no baseline worth the name. On this data the rule will
// mostly fire on YOU running the Lab F suite — which is useful for proving the
// rule works, and useless as a detection.
//
// The correct framing: this rule is real in a system with real traffic, and in
// a lab it is a demonstration of the technique. Say that rather than presenting
// a lab anomaly score as a finding. Overclaiming on a detection is the fastest
// way to lose a technical interviewer.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SIMPLER RULE THAT IS OFTEN BETTER
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// A fixed threshold beats anomaly detection whenever you can state the number,
// because it is explainable at 3am and it has no warm-up period:
//
// AzureDiagnostics
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)
// | where ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.COGNITIVESERVICES"
// | summarize Requests = count() by Resource, bin(TimeGenerated, 5m)
// | where Requests > 200     // set from YOUR observed normal, not from a guess
//
// Preferring the simple rule where it works is a judgement call worth making
// visible. The sophisticated rule is not automatically the better control.
